---- Creation design pattern-----------
This pattern deals with the process of object instance creation irrespective of it's implementation.

**Abstract Factory:-
		When a number of derived classes must need to define set of methods,
		we should define a parent class as abstract and should define common methods as abstract method. This is called abstract factory.

		Ex:-public abstract class A{
			public abstract void Method1();
			public abstract void Method2();
		}
		
		public class B: A{
			public virtual void Method1(){
				//some implementation
			}
			public virtual void Method2(){
				//some implementation
			}
		}
		
		public class C: A{
			public virtual void Method1(){
				//some implementation
			}
			public virtual void Method2(){
				//some implementation
			}
		}
		
*** Singleton
		This pattern is desined to create only one instance of a perticular class through out the application.
		Ex:-
		public sealed class Singleton
			{
				private static Singleton instance=null;

				private Singleton()
				{
				}

				public static Singleton Instance
				{
					get
					{
						if (instance==null)
						{
							instance = new Singleton();
						}
						return instance;
					}
				}
			}
			
		Ex:- Singleton with tread safe
		public sealed class Singleton
		{
			private static Singleton instance = null;
			private static readonly object padlock = new object();

			Singleton()
			{
			}

			public static Singleton Instance
			{
				get
				{
					lock (padlock)
					{
						if (instance == null)
						{
							instance = new Singleton();
						}
						return instance;
					}
				}
			}
		}
**** Factory Pattern
		In Factory pattern, we create the object without exposing the creation logic and initialization of object happens at runtime.
		
